I agree with David here.  The point is not to make the easy game
harder.  The point is to make the dificulty more consistent.

(Personally, I would even like to make the early game a little bit
easier on the average, but make it much more consistent, if I could. 
That would mean no unavoidable gnome-with-wand-of-death, and no
completely randomly generated game breaker like a 7 charge WoW.  I
can't think of a good way to fix the first, but the second can be
fixed.)

The point is to make the game more dependant on skill, and less
dependant on luck.  A game with an early wand of wishing is currently
much easier to win than a game without.  I don't think anyone is
denying that.  There are other combinations of items that you might
find that make the game much easier than usual, I don't think anyone
is denying that.  But the former is far more likely to occur, and has
a much larger impact on the variance of the difficulty.

When you set out to play a game of Nethack, it is a good thing to have
an idea that the game will have a certain difficulty.  It is a bad
thing that some sessions will be very hard, while others will be very
easy, entirely at random.  Look at Crawl (which is a fantastic game,
by the way).  The chief complaint people seem to have there is that
some games, you are doomed from the start.  That is one of the effects
of variance in difficulty.

This has to be balanced against the good aspects of randomness in
Nethack.  As far as I see it, the main benefits are that each game is
very different, and that there are some unexpected events that make
you say wow.  If we reduce the wishes on a WoW to a maximum of 3, both
of these benefits will be diminished.  But not by that much.  Who can
honestly say that an early WoW wouldn't be nearly as exciting even if
it only gave you 3 wishes?

And if it makes the game too hard, we can always adjust something else
that is a little more consistant to make it easier.

